(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-2-eye-exam-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Eye Examination — Set 2 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","questions":[{"q":"Before beginning the ophthalmoscopic exam, the examiner should darken the room, adjust the scope's light size/brightness, and turn the diopter dial to...","choices":["+20 diopters","0 diopter","there is no starting diopter setting","-20 diopters"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ophthalmoscope is initially set to 0 diopter before beginning the exam; if the examiner wears glasses, they should remove them and adjust the diopter setting to accommodate their own vision instead. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 32"},{"q":"To begin the ophthalmoscopic exam, the patient looks up over the examiner's shoulder while the examiner starts approximately how far away, at what angle?","choices":["1 inch away, directly in front of the patient's line of vision","15 inches away, at a 20-degree angle lateral to the patient's line of vision","5 feet away, standing directly behind the patient's shoulder","There is no recommended starting distance or angle to use"],"answer":1,"exp":"The examiner starts approximately 15 inches away from the patient, positioned at about a 20-degree angle lateral to the patient's line of vision, shining the light onto the pupil to note the red reflex before moving closer. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 33"},{"q":"When beginning the ophthalmoscopic exam from 15 inches away, the examiner shines the light onto the pupil and notes the...","choices":["visual acuity number","nothing in particular at this step","red reflex","pupil color exclusively, unrelated to any reflex"],"answer":2,"exp":"From the initial starting position, the examiner shines the light onto the pupil and notes the red reflex — a reflection off the vascular retina that should appear as a consistent reddish-orange glow; its absence or asymmetry can be an important abnormal finding. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 33"},{"q":"As the examiner moves closer during the ophthalmoscopic exam, the thumb of the opposite hand is placed...","choices":["nowhere in particular; hand position doesn't matter","over the patient's mouth","on the examiner's own forehead","across the patient's eyebrow"],"answer":3,"exp":"Placing the thumb of the opposite hand across the patient's eyebrow serves as a physical guide and stabilizer as the examiner moves in close, keeping the light focused on the red reflex until almost touching the thumb and the patient's eyelashes. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 34"},{"q":"During the fundoscopic exam, if the examiner follows a blood vessel toward the center of the retina until it meets the optic disc, the vessel should...","choices":["increase in diameter closer to the disc","disappear entirely before reaching the disc","decrease in diameter closer to the disc","change color from red to blue near the disc"],"answer":0,"exp":"As retinal blood vessels are traced toward the optic disc, they should progressively increase in diameter closer to the disc, reflecting the branching pattern of the retinal vasculature radiating outward from that point. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"When examining a nearsighted patient with the ophthalmoscope, the diopter dial should be rotated toward...","choices":["plus diopters (green numbers)","exactly 0 diopters regardless of the patient's refraction","it makes no difference for nearsighted patients","minus diopters (red numbers)"],"answer":3,"exp":"For a nearsighted (myopic) patient, the examiner rotates the diopter dial toward minus diopters (typically shown in red on the dial) to bring the fundus into focus. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"When examining a farsighted patient with the ophthalmoscope, the diopter dial should be rotated toward...","choices":["minus diopters (red numbers)","plus diopters (green numbers)","exactly 0 diopters regardless of the patient's refraction","it makes no difference for farsighted patients"],"answer":1,"exp":"For a farsighted (hyperopic) patient, the examiner rotates the diopter dial toward plus diopters (typically shown in green on the dial) to bring the fundus into focus. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"On fundoscopic inspection of the optic disc, the examiner assesses sharpness of the disc margins, color of the disc, and...","choices":["the patient's color vision score","the pupil's reaction to light","size of the physiologic cup","the patient's visual acuity number"],"answer":2,"exp":"Optic disc assessment includes evaluating the sharpness of its margins, its color, and the size of the physiologic cup (the central depression within the disc), all of which can be affected by various pathologic processes. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 36"},{"q":"On fundoscopic inspection of the retina, retinal arteries compared with veins characteristically appear...","choices":["Invisible entirely on fundoscopic exam, since only the optic disc and macula can normally be visualized directly","Identical to veins in color, caliber, and light reflection, with essentially no distinguishing features visible","Darker red and noticeably larger in diameter than veins, with a duller, more diffuse light reflection than veins","lighter red, smaller in diameter, with a bright light reflection, compared with darker red, larger veins with an inconspicuous/absent reflection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Retinal arteries are light red, smaller in diameter, and show a bright light reflection (from their thicker, more muscular walls), while veins are darker red, larger in diameter, and have an inconspicuous or absent light reflection. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 37"},{"q":"On fundoscopic inspection of the retina, besides arteries and veins, the examiner also inspects the arteriovenous crossings, fovea, and...","choices":["eyelashes","macula","iris","cornea"],"answer":1,"exp":"Retinal inspection includes the arteries, veins, arteriovenous crossings, fovea, and macula, together providing a comprehensive view of key posterior segment structures. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 37"},{"q":"To inspect anterior structures such as the vitreous and lens during the ophthalmoscopic exam, the examiner progressively adjusts the diopter to approximately...","choices":["+10 to +12","there is no way to inspect anterior structures with the ophthalmoscope","0 exactly, with no adjustment","-10 to -12"],"answer":0,"exp":"Inspecting the more anterior structures (vitreous, lens) during the ophthalmoscopic exam requires progressively adjusting the diopter to around +10 to +12, allowing the examiner to focus on structures closer to the front of the eye rather than the retina. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 39"},{"q":"The medial rectus muscle, innervated by cranial nerve III (oculomotor), functions to...","choices":["rotate the eye around its long axis (torsion) exclusively","have no role in eye movement","move the eye inward, toward the nose (adduction)","move the eye outward, away from the nose (abduction)"],"answer":2,"exp":"The medial rectus, innervated by CN (cranial nerve) III, adducts the eye (moves it inward, toward the nose) — the opposite action of the lateral rectus (CN (cranial nerve) VI), which abducts the eye. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"The inferior oblique muscle, innervated by cranial nerve III (oculomotor), functions to...","choices":["rotate the top of the eye away from the nose (extorsion) and also move the eye upward (elevation)","have essentially no measurable role in normal, coordinated extraocular eye movement","move the eye outward, away from the nose (abduction), with no vertical action","move the eye downward (depression) and rotate the top toward the nose (intorsion)"],"answer":0,"exp":"The inferior oblique, innervated by CN (cranial nerve) III, produces extorsion (rotating the top of the eye away from the nose) and also contributes to elevation of the eye — actions that oppose the superior oblique's intorsion and depression. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"Observing gaze deviation, nystagmus, or lid lag during extraocular movement testing can each be a sign of...","choices":["only a normal anatomic variant with no diagnostic significance whatsoever","underlying neurologic, muscular, or thyroid-related pathology, depending on the specific finding","exclusively a marker of decreased visual acuity, unrelated to any other pathology","nothing of clinical significance in any patient, regardless of the specific finding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Gaze deviation and nystagmus can reflect neurologic or muscular abnormalities affecting the extraocular muscles/cranial nerves, while lid lag is classically associated with thyroid eye disease (for example, hyperthyroidism) — each finding pointing toward a different, clinically meaningful category of underlying pathology. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 28"},{"q":"On inspection of the eyebrows, the examiner assesses symmetry, hair distribution/loss, and...","choices":["visual acuity","pupil size","scaling","color vision"],"answer":2,"exp":"Eyebrow inspection specifically includes assessing symmetry, hair distribution or loss, and scaling (which can suggest an underlying dermatologic condition such as seborrheic dermatitis). — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"On inspection of the eyelashes, the examiner assesses condition and...","choices":["pupil reaction to light","visual field extent","color vision","direction of growth"],"answer":3,"exp":"Eyelash inspection specifically includes assessing their condition and direction of growth, since an abnormal growth direction (for example, trichiasis, where lashes turn inward) can cause corneal irritation. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"On inspection of the lacrimal apparatus, the examiner assesses for swelling and...","choices":["visual acuity exclusively, nothing else","extraocular movements exclusively, alone","pupil size and reactivity exclusively","excessive tearing, discharge, or dryness"],"answer":3,"exp":"Lacrimal apparatus assessment includes checking for swelling as well as excessive tearing, discharge, or dryness, which can indicate problems with tear production or drainage. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"General inspection of the eye and surrounding structures begins with assessing the position, alignment, and...","choices":["width of the palpebral fissures","reaction of the pupils to light","the patient's visual acuity number","the patient's color vision score"],"answer":0,"exp":"Initial eye inspection includes assessing the position and alignment of the eyes as well as the width of the palpebral fissures (the opening between the upper and lower eyelids), before moving to more detailed assessment of the eyebrows, lashes, lids, and lacrimal apparatus. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"Pseudoisochromatic color plates are specifically useful for screening because they...","choices":["test visual acuity through a series of progressively smaller printed letters, not color perception","require the patient to read progressively smaller lines of printed text at near distance","use dots of subtly different but similarly-toned colors, which a color-vision-deficient patient cannot distinguish","use only black and white contrast dots that are unrelated to color discrimination at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"These plates embed a figure using dots of colors that differ subtly in hue but are similar in brightness/saturation, making the figure invisible or hard to distinguish for a patient with a color vision deficiency, while a patient with normal color vision can readily identify it. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 21"},{"q":"A visual field defect detected on confrontation testing would prompt referral for automated perimetry because confrontation testing...","choices":["is inherently more accurate and quantitative than formal automated perimetry in every case","requires no special equipment whatsoever, unlike automated perimetry which always does","is a screening test and cannot provide the detailed, quantitative mapping needed for a definitive diagnosis","always gives a completely normal result regardless of any underlying visual pathway lesion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Confrontation testing is a useful bedside screening tool, but it cannot provide the detailed, quantitative mapping of the visual field that automated perimetry (performed by an ophthalmologist) can, which is needed to definitively characterize and diagnose a visual field defect. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 19"},{"q":"Asking about corrective lenses before visual acuity testing is important because...","choices":["testing should be performed both with and without correction to fully characterize the patient's vision","only the patient's uncorrected vision is ever tested, regardless of glasses or contacts","only the patient's corrected vision is ever tested, ignoring baseline uncorrected acuity","corrective lenses have no bearing whatsoever on visual acuity testing or its results"],"answer":0,"exp":"Because a patient's corrected vision (with glasses/contacts) can differ substantially from uncorrected vision, testing both with and without correction (when applicable) gives a fuller picture of visual function. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 17"},{"q":"Ensuring the exam room is well-lit before visual acuity testing primarily helps to...","choices":["have no bearing whatsoever on the accuracy of the acuity result obtained","replace the need for a standardized eye chart during the assessment","intentionally dilate the patient's pupils before ophthalmoscopy","ensure the eye chart is clearly visible, producing an accurate assessment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Adequate room lighting ensures the eye chart itself is clearly visible and evenly illuminated, which is necessary for an accurate, reproducible visual acuity assessment. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 16"},{"q":"The lacrimal apparatus and its drainage system are responsible for...","choices":["producing and draining tears to keep the ocular surface moist","producing aqueous humor to maintain intraocular pressure","focusing incoming light rays precisely onto the retina","controlling pupil size in response to ambient light"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lacrimal apparatus produces tears, and its drainage system channels excess tears away (ultimately into the nasal cavity), together maintaining a moist, protected ocular surface. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"Aqueous humor, whose circulation is illustrated in the eye's anatomy, is best understood as...","choices":["the same watery fluid produced by the lacrimal gland that lubricates the ocular surface","a substance that is entirely unrelated to the regulation or measurement of intraocular pressure","a solid, crystalline structure located within the substance of the lens itself, not a fluid","a fluid produced and circulated within the anterior segment of the eye, helping maintain intraocular pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Aqueous humor is a fluid produced by the ciliary body that circulates through the anterior segment of the eye and drains via specific outflow pathways, playing a key role in maintaining normal intraocular pressure. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"The optic disc, inspected during the fundoscopic exam, is typically described as a...","choices":["completely invisible on fundoscopic exam","dark blue, irregularly-shaped structure","identical in appearance to the macula","round, yellow-orange structure"],"answer":3,"exp":"The optic disc is typically a round, yellow-orange structure visible on fundoscopic exam, representing the point where the optic nerve fibers and retinal blood vessels exit/enter the eye. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"When spreading the eyelids using thumb and index finger for a full view of the conjunctiva/sclera (if necessary), this technique is used specifically to...","choices":["improve visualization when the lids alone don't provide adequate exposure","test visual acuity using a standard handheld or wall eye chart","replace the need for the patient to look in different directions","assess the pupillary reaction to direct and consensual light"],"answer":0,"exp":"Manually spreading the eyelids provides additional exposure and a fuller view of the conjunctiva and sclera when simply asking the patient to look in different directions doesn't provide adequate visualization on its own. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"Placing a hand across the bridge of the nose while testing the consensual pupillary reaction serves to...","choices":["serve no functional purpose at all beyond simple patient comfort during testing","improve the patient's measured visual acuity during the pupillary light testing","block light from directly reaching the opposite eye, isolating the consensual response","directly measure the resting diameter of the tested pupil in millimeters"],"answer":2,"exp":"Placing a hand across the bridge of the nose blocks the light source from directly illuminating the opposite eye, ensuring that any constriction observed there is truly a consensual response (via the shared neural pathway) rather than a direct response to stray light. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"A visual acuity result of 20/20 would indicate that...","choices":["the visual acuity test could not be completed due to a technical error","the patient meets the formal clinical criteria for legal blindness","the patient has significantly below-average, subnormal visual acuity","the patient can read at 20 feet what a person with normal vision can also read at 20 feet"],"answer":3,"exp":"A 20/20 result means the patient's vision matches the normal reference standard — able to read at 20 feet the same line that a person with normal visual acuity can also read at 20 feet — representing normal visual acuity, not necessarily 'perfect' vision beyond normal. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 18"},{"q":"The visual field of each eye, illustrated in the anatomy review, refers to...","choices":["the full extent of space visible to that eye while it maintains fixation on a central point","only the very center of vision, with all peripheral vision completely excluded from view","identical between the left and right eyes in every patient, with no normal variation","simply another, less common way of measuring the patient's central visual acuity"],"answer":0,"exp":"The visual field represents the entire area of space an eye can see (peripherally and centrally) while fixed on a central point, forming the basis for visual field testing methods like confrontation testing. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"If an examiner who normally wears glasses removes them to use the ophthalmoscope, they should compensate by...","choices":["asking the patient to hold and operate the ophthalmoscope instead","there being no way to compensate for the examiner's own refractive error","performing the exam with blurred vision and making no compensation at all","adjusting the diopter setting on the scope to accommodate their own vision"],"answer":3,"exp":"Removing glasses to get closer to the patient during ophthalmoscopy is common practice; the examiner compensates for their own refractive error by adjusting the ophthalmoscope's diopter setting rather than wearing glasses during the exam. — Physical Examination of the Eye lecture, Slide 32"}]};
